# MOF Thin Film-Coated Metal Oxide Nanowire Array: Significantly Improved Chemiresistor Sensor Performance

## abstract
A strategy for combining metal oxides and metal-organic frameworks is proposed to design new materials for sensing volatile organic compounds, for the first time. The prepared ZnO@ZIF-CoZn core-sheath nanowire arrays show greatly enhanced performance not only on its selectivity but also on its response, recovery behavior, and working temperature.

As a critical family of chemiresistor materials, metal oxides (MOX), such as $z\mathrm{nO}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , have been developed for many industrial and domestic applications in volatile organic compound (VOC) detection due to their low cost, portability, real-time operability, and ease of use.[1] To pursue higher sensitivity, better selectivity, faster responsivity, and lower working temperature, nanostructured MOX materials have been extensively studied due to their increased surface-to-volume ratios and reactive surface areas compared with bulk.[1b,2] However, selectivity is still a major challenge for commercial MOX gas sensor devices, although several ways, such as cross-sensitivity adjustment, electronic noses and gas preseparation by other instrument, have been developed to address this issue.[3] For instance, water vapor is a typical interference of MOX gas sensor for VOC detecting, which commonly exists in the air with high concentration $(40{-}70~\mathrm{RH}\%$ , ${\approx}11$ 440–20 019 ppm) and will generate false responses to a major source for unreliable results.[4] Although many efforts like doping, p–n junctions, and noble metal decoration have been made to reduce the negative effects of humidity, an ideal solution is still in need.[4b,c]
Metal organic framework (MOF) is a class of crystalline framework-structured material constructed by connecting metal center with organic linker. Such materials feature regular pores, ultra large surface areas, tunable framework structures, and open-metal sites and demonstrate potential applications on catalysis, gas storage and separation, drug delivery, nanoscale reactors, proton conduction, etc.[5,6] Particularly, their selective gas adsorption behavior makes MOF very attractive for overcoming the selectivity problem in gas sensor.[7–9] For example, Zhang’s group reported two hydrophobic MOFs, $[{\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{im}})_{2}]_{n}$ and $[{\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{mim}})_{2}]_{n},$ exhibiting selective response to VOC gases without the interference of humidity.[7a,b] In contrast, Achmann et al. first found that hydrophilic MOF, Fe-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate, selectively shows higher response to humidity than VOCs.[8] Even so, the reported MOF-based chemiresistor sensors suffer from low sensitivity to analytes.[8–10]
Here, we propose a new material design strategy to improve the performance of chemiresistor gas sensor by combining the high sensitivity of nanostructured MOX with the high selectivity and catalytic activity of MOF into one material. First, MOX nanowire is used as core material for gas sensing reaction and subsequent electrical signal transport. Then, by coating a layer of MOF thin film on the surface of MOX nanowire, a $\operatorname{MOX}({\overline{{a}}})$ MOF core–sheath nanowire material could be obtained. In such a core–sheath nanostructured material, the porous sheath material is used to selectively usher desired target species and reject interfering gases from passing through to reach MOX surface. By this manner, the selectivity of MOX gas sensor could be greatly improved. Moreover, catalytic properties of MOF may also be introduced into such materials to enhance their sensing performance.
For the first time, the above described strategy was successfully demonstrated by coating a layer of hydrophobic and catalytic Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework - CoZn (ZIF-CoZn, isostructural with ZIF-8 $^{3}(Z\mathrm{n})$ or ZIF-67(Co)) thin film on ZnO to form a core–sheath nanowire array for chemiresistor gas sensor, which can improve the performance on detecting acetone under humidity interference. The detection of ppb/ppmlevel acetone is of great essence for environmental monitoring and clinic application.[11] On one hand, acetone is one of most used solvents and reagents in industry and laboratory. It can anaesthetize the central nervous system of human and cause damage to kidney, pancreas, and liver. On the other hand, acetone is an important breath biomarker for diagnosing diabetes, because its concentration would increase from 3 to 9 ppm in health human body to more than 18 ppm in diabetic patients. Breath acetone analysis was found to be more effective than urine sampling for monitoring ketosis for insulin-dependent diabetic patients.[12] However, the humidity in environment and human breath can vary within a broad range, and the crosssensitivity of humidity may plague the precise detection of acetone by MOX sensors due to their intrinsically poor selectivity. We found that $\mathrm{ZnO@\mathbb{Q}I F{\cdot}C o Z n}$ nanowire array-based sensor shows greatly enhanced selectivity of acetone to humidity, much better response, accelerated response and recovery behavior, and significantly decreased working temperature, compared with $\mathrm{znO}$ nanowire array-based sensor.
The $\mathrm{ZnO@\mathbb{Q}I F{\cdot}C o Z n}$ gas sensor was prepared by coating a layer of ZIF-CoZn thin film on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanowire array via a simple solution method (for details see Figure 1 and Table S1, Supporting Information). Notably the synthesis of MOF-coated metal oxide nanorod has been reported by others.[13] In their work, metal oxide nanorod works as sacrificial template to supply $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ for constructing MOF. Differently, besides $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ dissolved from $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod, foreign ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ source was also involved in the synthesis of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ . The competition between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ to coordinate to organic ligand results the control synthesis of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\varnothing\mathrm{ZIF-CoZn}}$ much more complex. Moreover, in order to obtain higher aspect ratio for better sensing performance, thinner $\mathrm{znO}$ nanowire $(50-100~\mathrm{~nm})$ was used than that $(600\pm100~\mathrm{nm})$ ) in the previous work. All these make it more difficult to control the thickness of MOF thin film. All factors in the reaction, such as temperature, the ratio of N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) and water, the concentration of organic ligand and metal ion, were found to vary the final thickness of MOF sheath in $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ nanostructure. After optimization, by fixing the reaction conditions with DMF and water in 13.5:2.5 ratio, $0.86\times10^{-3}$ m $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , ZIF-CoZn thin film can be controlled to grow to required thickness by only changing the concentration of 2-methylimadazole (Hmim, details see the Supporting Information).
Figure 2a,e shows the morphological features of $z\mathrm{nO}$ nanowire array with the length of $2{\mathrm{-}}4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and the diameter of $50{-}100~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. All the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanowires (Figure 2a,e) are single crystal and grow along [001] direction. After coated with a layer of ZIF-CoZn (Figure 2b–d,f–h), the nanowires keep standing vertically on the substrate with similar length. But their diameters become larger as increasing the thickness of ZIF-CoZn sheath. Scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) measurements (Figure S1, Supporting Information) clearly show the core–sheath structure of $\mathrm{ZnO@\mathbb{Q}I F{\cdot}C o Z n}$ . $z\mathrm{nO}$ core shows brighter contrast owing to larger atomic mass than that of MOF sheath (Figure S1c–e, Supporting Information). Although, it is not easy to clearly observe the sheath with only 5 and $15~\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness, elemental maps of these samples demonstrate that Co and $Z\mathrm{n}$ do coexist in sheath. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements further confirm the coexistence of Co and $Z\mathrm{n}$ but also show that the distribution of Co and $Z\mathrm{n}$ in ZIF-CoZn sheath is not homogenous (Figure S2, Supporting Information). It has been known that XPS is a surface-sensitive quantitative spectroscopic technique. Normally it can detect the elements as deep as $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ from the surface. Moreover, XPS measurements reveal that both metals are $^{2+}$ . This valence is just right for compensating the negative charge of Hmim ligand in ZIF-CoZn, which can also be found in ZIF-8(Zn) and ZIF-67(Co).[14] N1s peak of ZIF-CoZn sheath located between those of ZIF$8(\mathrm{Zn})$ and ZIF-67(Co) (Figure S2d, Supporting Information), which indicates the coexistence of $\mathrm{Co-N}$ and $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ bonds in ZIF-CoZn. In addition, the order of N1s binding energy, $\mathrm{Co-ZIF<ZnO@100~nm~ZIF-CoZn<ZnO@15~nm}$ ZIF-CoZn $<Z\mathrm{n}$ -ZIF indicates that the ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ on the surface of ZIF-CoZn sheath increased with increasing the thickness of sheath. This may be due to the concentration gradient of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ from the surrounding of $\mathsf{Z n O}$ nanowire to reaction solution during synthesis. The peaks of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ in powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) before $20^{\circ}$ are very similar to these of ZIF-8(Zn) and ZIF-67(Co) (Figure S3, Supporting Information), which demonstrates that ZIF-CoZn is isostructural with ZIF-8 $(\mathsf{Z n})$ and ZIF-67(Co).[13,15] Besides the peaks of ZIF-CoZn, other peaks in PXRD pattern could be assigned to wurtzite $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (Joint Committee on Powder Diffraction Standards, JCPDS Card No. 01-075-1526) and sapphire substrate (Figure S3b, Supporting Information). As the thickness of sheath increases, the intensity of the diffraction peaks of ZIFCoZn also increases that further confirms the results of scanning electron microscopy (SEM), TEM, and XPS analysis.
Gas sensing performance of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ sensor was evaluated with $\mathsf{Z n O}$ nanowire array-based sensor as control sample. The experiments were carried out by placing the sensor device in a sealed quartz chamber of tube furnace and monitoring the resistance of the device in diluted acetone with dry air as carrier gas (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Figure 3a shows the typical dynamic response of $\mathrm{ZnO}@Z\mathrm{IF}$ - CoZn sensor to acetone with different concentration in dry air and to $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ acetone under different relative humidity (RH) as interference. The current curve of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ sensor presents good response recovery to a broad range of the acetone concentrations $(0.25{-}100~\mathrm{\ppm})$ . Notably, after coating with a layer of ZIF-CoZn, the selectivity between acetone and humidity of $\mathrm{znO}$ -based sensor has been significantly improved.
The coefficient of variation (CV) is used to represent the effect of humidity on responses, which is defined as
$$
\mathrm{CV}=R_{\mathrm{SD}}/R_{\mathrm{average}}\times100\%
$$
where $R_{\mathrm{SD}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{average}}$ are the standard deviation (SD) and average value of responses with different humidity, respectively. The higher CV value means the worse antiinterference performance. At $10~\mathrm{ppm}$ acetone, the responses of the $\mathrm{ZnO@5~nm}$ ZIF-CoZn device only varies $7.42\%$ (CV) when changing RH from $0\%$ to $90\%$ (Figure 3a,b). Comparatively, $\mathsf{Z n O}$ nanowire array sensor shows a variation as high as $31.76\%$ at the same conditions (Figure 3b). To increase the thickness of ZIF-CoZn thin film from 5 to $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ would further decrease the CV to $6.53\%$ . However, when further increasing ZIF-CoZn thin film to $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , the selectivity of the device becomes worse and CV as huge as $41.01\%$ was observed.
The sensor response in this work is defined as the ratio of sensor resistance in air $\left(R_{\mathrm{air}}\right)$ and in analytic gas $(R_{\mathrm{analyte}})$ :
$$
\mathrm{Response}=R_{\mathrm{air}}/R_{\mathrm{analyte}}-1
$$
Response-concentration log–log plots show good linearity for all sensors, based on which the detection limit of each sensor can be deduced by setting Response $=0.1$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}\ @5\ \mathrm{{\nm}}}$ ZIF-CoZn device has the best detection limit in all devices, which is down to trace level with the value of $0.0019~\mathrm{ppm}$ . The detection limits of the devices in this work are in the order of $\mathrm{ZnO@5~nm~ZIF{\mathrm{-}}C o Z n}$ $<\mathrm{ZnO@15~nm~ZIF-CoZn<ZnO<ZnO100~nm}$ ZIF-CoZn. Compared with $\mathsf{Z n O}$ nanowire array sensor, $Z_{\mathrm{nO}@{\mathbb O}}5~\mathrm{nm}$ ZIFCoZn has improved the detection limit by almost 2 orders of magnitude (Table 1) and this value is comparable with that of noble metal decorated $z\mathrm{nO}$ gas sensors working at higher temperature (Table S2, Supporting Information).
In order to optimize the operating temperature for best sensing response, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ sensors were evaluated from 200 to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $10\mathrm{ppm}$ acetone atmosphere (Figure 3c). Compared with $z\mathrm{nO}$ nanowire array sensor which has optimal operating temperature around $375^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in this work, ZIFCoZn thin film coating decrease the optimal operating temperature of the sensors to $240{-}260~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Notably, the response of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}\textcircled{a}5}$ nm ZIF-CoZn sensor to $10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ acetone is up to 27 even at $260~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is ${\approx}20$ times higher than that of pure $\mathrm{znO}$ nanowire array (only 1.5). Moreover, the response of $\mathrm{ZnO@5~nm~ZIF{\cdot}C o Z n}$ sensor to $100~\mathrm{ppm}$ acetone is much higher than that of noble metal decorated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ sensors and comparable to the best reported $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ -based sensor (Table S2, Supporting Information).
The response time of the sensor is the time required increasing the current to $90\%$ of the saturation value and the recovery time is the time required decreasing the saturated current to its $10\%$ . Figure 3d illustrates the response and recovery characteristic curves of the sensors based on instantaneous supply and cutoff of $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ acetone in a $10\ \mathrm{min}$ cycle. $\mathrm{ZnO@5~nm~ZIF{\cdot}C o Z n}$ shows the shortest response and recovery times in all sensors of this work with the values of 0.72 and $1.02\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. Compared with $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanowire array sensor, the response and recovery of $Z_{\mathrm{nO}@{\mathbb O}5}~\mathrm{nm}$ ZIFCoZn speed up by $48\%$ and $470\%$ , respectively.
$\mathrm{ZnO@5~nm~ZIF{\cdot}C o Z n}$ also shows good response repeatability and recyclability to acetone (for details see Figure S6,
Supporting Information). After continuously working for 1 week, the sensor can still keep CV less than $10\%$ . Moreover, besides acetone, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ sensor shows responses to many other VOCs and gases (Figure S7, Supporting Information), which makes it possible to use $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\varnothing\mathrm{ZIF-CoZn}}$ to solve the humidity problem in detecting these analytes.
Vapor sorption measurements confirm that porous ZIFCoZn $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{Co};\mathrm{Zn}=1{:}1$ in mole) can selectively adsorb acetone rather than water (Figure S8a, Supporting Information). PXRD patterns show that this sample has the same crystal structure as that of $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ and ZIF-67(Co) (Figure S8b, Supporting Information). ZIF-CoZn coating on the surface of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanowire can serve as a filtration membrane to allow acetone molecule in and refuse the entry of water molecule due to its hydrophobic nature. So, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZIF-CoZn}$ -based sensors show good selectivity between acetone and humidity (Figure 3a,b). The poor antihumidity of $\mathrm{ZnO@200~nm}$ ZIF-CoZn as shown in Figure 3b can be explained by the competition response of n-type $z\mathrm{nO}$ core and p-type $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ sheath. It has been mentioned above that ZIF-CoZn, ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ and ZIF-67(Co) are isostructural. However, the thermogravimetric analysis - differential thermal analysis (TG-DTA) measurements of these three samples show that the more Zn content in the crystal structure would result the better thermal stability (Figure S8c, Supporting Information). Therefore, ZIF-8 $(\mathsf{Z n})$ shows the highest thermal stability and keeps intact until $330^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ZIF-67 (Co) shows the worst thermal stability and can only keep its structure to $220~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while ZIF-CoZn has medium thermal stability between 220 and $330^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ On the other hand, it can be observed from XPS measurements that with the thickness of MOF thin film increase, ${\mathrm{Co}}{:}\mathrm{Zn}$ ratio on the surface of MOF thin film also increases (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Therefore, $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ZIF-CoZn coating has the worst thermal stability than 5 and $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ ones. After measured at $260~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{ZnO@\underline{{\alpha}}100~n m}$ ZIF-CoZn shows its color changes from purple to black which is due to the pyrolysis of ZIF-CoZn to form $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ nanoparticles, which can be confirmed by TEM measurement (Figure S9, Supporting Information). As known, $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ is a typical p-type semiconductor and can be used as active material for detecting acetone.[14a,16] But similar to other MOX, $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ is also sensitive to humidity (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the competition of n-type $z\mathrm{nO}$ core and continuous p-type ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{x}$ sheath of $7\mathrm{nO}\ @100$ nm ZIF-CoZn decrease its response as observed in Figure 3c,d. Increasing ZIF-CoZn coating thicker to become a continuous thin film would even turn the device from n-type into p-type (Figure S11, Supporting Information) which completely destroys the selectivity of the sensor. Notably,
